+# lab/bio4-2026-11: a fourth set of mechanisms taken from biology
+
+From 145dd52. Host: the WSL2 guest of the Ryzen host (12 threads, Go 1.24.7).
+Each arm's rule is committed before its reading. The arms and postulates of
+lab/bio-2026-11 (B1 scent marks, B3 a fovea for hot entries),
+lab/bio2-2026-11 (B4 spike timing, B5 larval slots; B6 a lek for hot
+entries, B7 growth by segments) and lab/bio3-2026-11 (B8 despotic placement,
+B9 the cuckoo chick; B10 a reclaimer with a deadline, B11 a snapshot that
+rebases) are not repeated.
+
+## The structure as a sense organ: parts not yet matched
+
+lab/bio2's table read the structure as an organism, part by part, and
+lab/bio3's as a habitat that keys compete for. This one reads it as a sense
+organ: what it resolves, when it decides, and what its older parts are still
+for.
+
+| Part | What it does | Its counterpart in nature | Where the counterpart does better |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `TouchIfUnder`'s strict recipe: refuse while `Upper` is at the limit | `Upper` counts the bucket a window's edge cuts as whole, so a strict limiter admits 30–65% of a limit on 1–5 s windows (paper §4.2) | a hunter that samples the world at one rate, whatever is at stake | an echolocating bat calls about ten times a second while it searches and about two hundred in the last moments of an attack (the feeding buzz): resolution is bought when a decision turns on it, and dropped after (B12) |
+| the 4-bit epoch beside the register | says which unit word1 is aligned to while a touch has moved word0 and not yet word1 | the reptile's jaw joint: two bones that carry the hinge | when mammals got a second jaw joint the old hinge's bones had no load left, and became the ossicles of the middle ear: hearing got finer from parts a new joint made idle. Where both words move in one step (A33's 16-byte CAS) the epoch carries nothing (B13) |
+| the overflow table's sweep: every bucket visited when its epoch moves | keeps 2-bit slices unambiguous; 3.4–6.8 ms in one call on a 1M-key filter | a deciduous tree: every leaf at once, and bare for a season | an evergreen sheds by cohort: a needle carries its own age and the oldest cohort falls a little each year (B14, postulate) |
+| `TouchIfUnder`: a count, then a touch, under one of 64 locks | the decision makes two walks to the slot | a decision sent to the brain and back | a reflex arc: the knee decides at the first synapse (B15, postulate) |
+| a hot entry is held until its newest event is a horizon old | an idle heavy key keeps 64 B | muscle kept at full size | disuse atrophy: tissue that carries no load is resorbed, by the body and not at the muscle's request (B16, postulate) |
+| `alt`'s multiplier, 2^64 divided by the golden ratio | spreads a fingerprint's alternate lines | phyllotaxis: leaves set at the golden angle, so none shades the next | — (the same number, for the same reason) |
+| the stash, the overflow table and the hot entries are allocated on first use | a filter that is never attacked holds only its table | induced defences: a plant makes its toxin after the first bite | — |
+| the wait room and its placer; ConstantTime's pads that place waiting keys | the cuckoo search runs when the caller is not waiting on it | CAM plants take in carbon dioxide at night and spend it by day | — |
+
+## B12 The feeding buzz
+
+**The analogy.** An echolocating bat does not sample at one rate. Searching,
+it calls about ten times a second; once it has a target and the strike is a
+few hundred milliseconds away it calls about two hundred times a second, and
+then stops. It pays for resolution only where a decision turns on it. The
+filter's two earlier foveas put resolution where the traffic is: lab/bio's B3
+on the heaviest keys (a hot entry's newest units in sub-unit buckets) and
+lab/bio2's B4 on the lightest (a register that holds times while its key has
+six events or fewer in a horizon). Between them are the keys a rule like "5
+in 10 s" is written for: too busy for B4, far too light for a hot entry. What
+marks them out is not their rate. It is that a decision about them was made
+in doubt.
+
+**What a doubtful decision is.** `TouchIfUnder` refuses while `Upper` is at
+the limit. `Upper` counts the bucket the window's edge cuts as whole. A
+refusal is *uncertain* when the events certainly inside the window (the
+buckets wholly inside it, and the last touch) are under the limit: the
+register cannot say whether the key is over. Every false denial is an
+uncertain one.
+
+**The mechanism** (`TF_BUZZ=1`, `buzz.go`). On an uncertain refusal the key
+gets a *buzz entry*: 64 B in a side table of one entry per 128 slots (the
+hot table's size), allocated on the first one. It holds the key's identity,
+the slot's two words as they are then, and from then on the time of every
+event `TouchIfUnder` admits for the key: the newest 16, as 16-bit steps of
+Horizon/32768 (1.8 ms at a 60 s horizon), and the tick before which the log
+may be missing events (its start, then the newest event it has dropped).
+
+- **Trust.** An entry is trusted only while the key's slot is bit for bit
+  what the entry's last admission left (the displaced bit aside: a
+  relocation flips it). Every event the filter counts for the key changes
+  one of the two words, so while they match, the events since the entry's
+  start are exactly the logged ones. A plain `Touch`, a promotion to a hot
+  entry, an eviction whose history is shed: each changes a word, the entry
+  is dropped, and the key reads as it does today. After an admission the
+  slot must hold exactly what one event at `now` makes of the words read
+  before it (`nextReg`); if a plain touch slipped in between, it does not,
+  and the entry is dropped.
+- **The decision.** While an entry is trusted and the window starts after
+  the tick the log may be missing events before, the count is the log's: an
+  event is in the window unless its step ends before the window does
+  (`Upper`), to which the overflow table's part of `Upper` is added as
+  today. A limit of 16 or less never needs an event the log has dropped: if
+  fewer than the limit of the newest 16 are inside the window, the next
+  older one is outside it. Otherwise (the window reaches before the log's
+  start) the decision is the register's, as today, and the entry logs the
+  admission.
+- **Release.** A way is taken from an entry whose newest event is older than
+  the window it was given for and eight units more; a key with no way free
+  is refused an entry and reads as today.
+
+The register is kept as it is for every other caller; nothing on the hit
+path, the count path or a plain touch reads the table. ConstantTime filters
+keep `TouchIfUnder` as it is.
+
+**Why it cannot admit past a limit.** A trusted entry's log holds every
+event counted for the key since its start; a window that starts after the
+start (and after the newest dropped event) holds logged events only, each
+counted unless certainly outside. So the log's count is at least the
+truth, and a request is admitted only under the limit, as `Upper`
+guarantees today. The one way a counted event leaves no trace in the two
+words is the documented shedding of a history under `SpillMin`, which
+`Upper` does not see either.
+
+**Readings** (`TestLabBuzz`, `TF_LAB=1`; the filter itself, deterministic
+counts, nothing timed). Three strict limiters on the same offered Poisson
+traffic, as B3 and B4 read theirs: an exact sliding log, `TouchIfUnderAt`
+as it is (base), and the same with the buzz on. Rules (window/limit) on a
+60 s horizon: 1 s/5, 5 s/10, 10 s/3, 10 s/5, 30 s/10, 60 s/6, 60 s/16;
+offered 0.8x, 1.2x and 2x the limit; 20 horizons. Scenarios:
+
+- A: 400 contested keys in a filter of Capacity 200,000 (1,838 entries);
+- B: the same 400 keys in a filter of Capacity 12,800 (117 entries: 3.4
+  contested keys an entry);
+- C: A, with one request in ten a plain `TouchAt` (it counts, and no rule
+  is asked);
+- D: 1,000 contested keys (0.5% of Capacity, as B9's S1) beside tfbench's
+  churn stream at loads 1.00 and 1.15, so relocations, evictions and the
+  stash move the contested keys (4 horizons, the stream's length; rules
+  1 s/5 and 10 s/5);
+- E: limits past the log, 2.5 s/20 and 5 s/40 (reported, not judged).
+
+**Rule (before any reading).** Worth building if:
+
+1. no arm admits a request while the key's window already holds the limit,
+   asserted at every admission against the arm's own events, in every
+   scenario (in C the plain touches count);
+2. A, offered 2x: the buzz admits at least 0.97 of what the exact log
+   admits, for every rule;
+3. A, offered 0.8x: its false denials (refused while the key's own events in
+   the window are under the limit) are at most a quarter of the base's, for
+   every rule;
+4. B: the buzz admits no less than the base in any row (at least 0.99 of the
+   base's admissions);
+5. C: the buzz admits no less than the base (0.99);
+6. D: at load 1.00, offered 2x, at least 0.95 of the exact log; at load
+   1.15 no less than the base (0.99);
+7. `go test -short` passes with `TF_BUZZ=1`, plain and with `TF_BG=1`, and
+   the `TouchIf` tests pass under the race detector with it;
+8. memory: nothing until the first uncertain refusal, then at most 0.6 B per
+   key at design load (`Bytes()`).
+
+A rule's row is void if the base already admits 0.90 or more of the exact
+log at 2x (nothing to fix). Not read: speed (a conditional touch is already a
+lock, a count and a touch; the buzz adds a second read of the slot and one
+line of the table), `Resizable`, a model of the trust rule under
+concurrency.
